#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7304737-cba6-5fbc-b8be-0f1ac860d786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076888} {:answer/id #uuid "27e19e5e-9ae9-55e3-a6b1-2d3041c09690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 + π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076889} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa18fed-eec9-5887-a749-9f71fb14a6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076890} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8ad594-e424-5b7f-9948-5e8da6618757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076891} {:answer/id #uuid "33d74abb-4ac1-5932-857d-5df1fe3e33ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7304737-cba6-5fbc-b8be-0f1ac860d786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076888} :answer-field/id #uuid "6463ff6a-e667-5122-8f6a-66c6d265d077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b82eafa4-95ed-5313-a838-9f9ba69b6d8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49176" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/83b23d8138091a931d14854b197752d25d8149ee8914a70b8db73b6c2369c888.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
